# Eased motion

These are the motion rules and values Eased components are built with. Use them for any animation you add, so everything in the project moves the same way.

## If Eased components are installed, use them

Check for `components/eased/` in the project. If it exists:

- Use the installed Eased component for the job (Button, Popover, Tooltip, Tabs, Segmented, Accordion, Text Swap and so on). Don't build a second animated version beside it.
- Don't wrap Eased components in extra animation. Their motion is already tuned, and a second transition fights it.
- Import the shared values from `components/eased/lib/motion.ts` (`snappy`, `smooth`, `bouncy`, `ease`, `duration`, `stagger`, `enter`, `exit`, `reducedFade`, `useSafeReducedMotion`) and press classes from `components/eased/lib/press.ts` (`press`, `pressIcon`, `pressWide`). Don't retype the numbers.

If a component you need isn't installed, add it with the shadcn CLI. Eased is in the shadcn registry index under `@eased`:

```bash
npx shadcn add @eased/button
```

Or by URL:

```bash
npx shadcn add https://eased.dev/r/button.json
```

Free components include accordion, avatar, badge, breadcrumb, button, carousel, code-block, code-tabs, date-picker, dropdown-menu, file-upload, input, kbd, number-roll, pagination, popover, progress, radio-group, rating, segmented, skeleton, snippet, spinner, tabs, text-swap, textarea, toggle-group, tooltip and tree. The full list is at https://eased.dev/llms.txt.

If Eased isn't installed and the user doesn't want it, use the values below directly.

## When to animate

Motion is for feedback and continuity:

- Feedback on a press, toggle or selection.
- Something appearing from, or returning to, the thing that opened it.
- A value or label changing in place.
- A height or layout change the user caused.

## When not to animate

- Keyboard-driven, high-frequency actions: opening a command menu or context menu, adding or removing list items, typing.
- Layout the user didn't trigger.
- Hover never changes size, weight or position. It changes colour or shadow by one step.
- Never animate `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`, `padding` or `box-shadow` frame by frame. Animate only `transform`, `opacity`, `clip-path`, `filter` (blur up to 8px) and colours.
- Never use `transition: all`.
- Set `will-change` only during a gesture, and remove it after. Never put it in a stylesheet.

## Springs

Use only these three.

| Spring | Motion (motion/react) | CSS duration | Use for |
|---|---|---|---|
| `snappy` | `{ type: "spring", stiffness: 520, damping: 38 }` | 350ms | Presses, toggles, thumbs, indicators, text swaps, rolling digits, the focus ring |
| `smooth` | `{ type: "spring", visualDuration: 0.4, bounce: 0 }` | 450ms | Height and layout changes, FLIP, panels, toast and tooltip position. No overshoot. |
| `bouncy` | `{ type: "spring", stiffness: 400, damping: 26 }` | 600ms | Shape morphs and playful reveals only. Never on an exit. |

In CSS, the same springs are `linear()` curves. Pair each one with its duration:

```css
:root {
  --spring-snappy: linear(0, 0.0347, 0.1182, 0.2269, 0.3441, 0.4591, 0.5654, 0.6594, 0.7398, 0.8064, 0.8603, 0.9028, 0.9355, 0.96, 0.9778, 0.9902, 0.9986, 1.0039, 1.007, 1.0084, 1.0088, 1.0084, 1.0077, 1.0067, 1.0057, 1.0047, 1.0038, 1.0029, 1);
  --spring-smooth: linear(0, 0.0323, 0.1079, 0.2039, 0.3059, 0.4053, 0.4974, 0.5799, 0.6519, 0.7138, 0.7661, 0.8099, 0.8462, 0.8761, 0.9006, 0.9205, 0.9366, 0.9495, 0.9599, 0.9683, 0.9749, 0.9802, 0.9844, 0.9877, 0.9904, 0.9924, 0.9941, 0.9954, 1);
  --spring-bouncy: linear(0, 0.0758, 0.2474, 0.4501, 0.6424, 0.8022, 0.9212, 1.0003, 1.0455, 1.0651, 1.0673, 1.0593, 1.0464, 1.0326, 1.0202, 1.0103, 1.0032, 0.9987, 0.9963, 0.9954, 0.9956, 0.9963, 0.9972, 0.9981, 0.9989, 0.9995, 1, 1.0002, 1);
}

.thumb {
  transition: translate 350ms var(--spring-snappy);
}
```

With Eased installed these are `--eased-spring-snappy` with `--eased-duration-spring-snappy`, and so on, or the Tailwind classes `ease-spring-snappy`, `ease-spring-smooth` and `ease-spring-bouncy`.

## Curves

| Name | Value | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| enter | `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` | Things that appear |
| standard | `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` | Colour and opacity while something stays on screen |
| exit | `cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)` | Things that leave |
| in-out | `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)` | Movement that starts and ends on screen |

## Durations

| Name | Value | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| instant | 100ms | Press-down and the reduced-motion fade |
| fast | 150ms | Hover and colour changes |
| standard | 200ms | Popovers and tooltips |
| slow | 300ms | Dialogs |
| sheet | 500ms | Sheets and drawers |

Interaction feedback never takes longer than 200ms.

## Enter and exit

Exits are faster than enters. Something that appears is something to look at. Something that leaves is in the way. So an exit:

- takes at most three quarters of the enter time
- uses the exit (ease-in) curve
- never overshoots, so never use `bouncy` on an exit

Values Eased uses:

- Enter: 200ms on the enter curve. Fade in, rise 6px toward the trigger, scale from 0.97.
- Exit: 150ms on the exit curve. Fade out, travel 3px, scale to 0.98.
- Small things that leave (buttons, chips, the outgoing half of a swap): 100ms on the exit curve.

```tsx
<motion.div
  initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 6, scale: 0.97 }}
  animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0, scale: 1, transition: { duration: 0.2, ease: [0.16, 1, 0.3, 1] } }}
  exit={{ opacity: 0, y: 3, scale: 0.98, transition: { duration: 0.15, ease: [0.7, 0, 0.84, 0] } }}
/>
```

- Anything that opens from a trigger grows from it. Set `transform-origin` to the trigger.
- Nothing scales up from 0.
- Make open and close interruptible: CSS transitions with `@starting-style`, or Motion's `animate`, so reversing mid-flight is smooth. Use `@keyframes` only for entrances that play once on mount.

## Press

Press down in 100ms, release on `snappy`. The scale depends on width, so a wide bar doesn't dip further than a small icon.

| Target | Scale |
|---|---|
| Icon buttons up to 48px | 0.96 |
| Text buttons | 0.97 |
| Bars and cards wider than 220px | 0.985 |

Triggers that open a popover or menu never scale. They press by colour instead.

## Hover

Apply hover styles only on devices with a fine pointer, so touch doesn't get stuck hover states:

```css
@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
  .row:hover { /* colour or shadow change, one step */ }
}
```

With Eased installed, use the `hover-fine:` and `group-hover-fine:` variants. Touch gets an `:active` colour instead.

## Swapping a label or icon in place

For changes like Save to Saved, or a copy icon to a check: the old item moves up 3px and the new one rises from 4px below, each with a 4px blur. The movement uses `snappy`. Opacity and blur tween separately at 150ms on the standard curve, so the blur clears before the movement settles.

```tsx
<AnimatePresence mode="popLayout" initial={false}>
  <motion.span
    key={label}
    initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 4, filter: "blur(4px)" }}
    animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0, filter: "blur(0px)" }}
    exit={{ opacity: 0, y: -3, filter: "blur(4px)" }}
    transition={{
      type: "spring", stiffness: 520, damping: 38,
      opacity: { duration: 0.15, ease: [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1] },
      filter: { duration: 0.15, ease: [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1] },
    }}
  >
    {label}
  </motion.span>
</AnimatePresence>
```

## Stagger

| Unit | Step |
|---|---|
| Character | 16ms |
| List item | 35ms |
| Word | 40ms |
| Line | 80ms |

Keep the whole stagger under 400ms. It runs once when the list mounts, never on a re-render.

## Indicators

Tab underlines, segmented thumbs and selections glide to the new item with shared layout (`layoutId`) on `snappy`. The items under them never scale or change weight.

## Reduced motion

Every animation needs a reduced branch. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, movement becomes a 100ms opacity fade or nothing, and looping animations stop.

- In CSS, use `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` (the `reduced:` variant with Eased installed).
- In React, read the setting after hydration, not during the first render, or reduced-motion visitors get a hydration mismatch. With Eased installed, use `useSafeReducedMotion()` from `components/eased/lib/motion.ts` rather than Motion's `useReducedMotion`. Without it:

```tsx
import { useSyncExternalStore } from "react";

const REDUCE = "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)";

export function useSafeReducedMotion() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    (onChange) => {
      const mq = window.matchMedia(REDUCE);
      mq.addEventListener("change", onChange);
      return () => mq.removeEventListener("change", onChange);
    },
    () => window.matchMedia(REDUCE).matches,
    () => false,
  );
}

const reduced = useSafeReducedMotion();
<motion.div layout transition={reduced ? { duration: 0.1 } : { type: "spring", stiffness: 520, damping: 38 }} />
```

- Loops pause off-screen and under reduced motion. Anything that autoplays for more than 5 seconds gets a pause control.

## Before you finish

- [ ] Every animation uses one of the three springs or a named curve and duration.
- [ ] Exits are at most three quarters of their enter, on the exit curve, with no overshoot.
- [ ] Nothing animates layout properties frame by frame, and there's no `transition: all`.
- [ ] Hover styles apply only on fine pointers and never change size or position.
- [ ] Every animation has a reduced-motion branch, read after hydration.
- [ ] If Eased components are installed, they're used, and none is wrapped in extra animation.

For layout, spacing, corners, colour and type rules as well, use the full Eased design skill: `npx shadcn add https://eased.dev/r/eased-skill.json`. Motion docs: https://eased.dev/docs/motion
